Jorge Llamb=EDas wrote:
> On Fri, Oct 8, 2010 at 5:48 AM, MorphemeAddict <lytlesw@gmail.com> wrote:
>=20
>>{mo'a} is a rafsi for {morna}.
>>
>>mo'a  too few digit/number: too few; subjective
>>
>>The following four gismu have the letters m-o-a in that order:
>>
>>mokca 'point' x1 is a point/instant/moment [0-dimensional shape/form]
>>in/on/at time/place x2
>>
>>mosra 'friction' x1 is friction [force opposing motion] due to
>>contact/rubbing between x2 and x3
>>
>>morna 'pattern' x1 is/reflects/represents a pattern of forms/events x2
>>arranged according to structure x3
>>
>>smoka 'sock' x1 is a sock/stocking [flexible foot and lower leg garment] =
of
>>material x2
>=20
>=20
> I believe "mo'a" was half inspired by "mleca", but not all cmavo
> necessarily have a gismu based source, some are more or less random.

Actually, very few are "random" per se.  There are huge swaths that were=20
inspired by particular gismu.  Then there were some that were created to=20
form mnemonic sequences (e.g. numbers, ko'V, vo'V), where they were=20
constrained by what was still available.  Probably only 50-100 cmavo or=20
fewer were assigned more or less randomly, because what was left offered=20
nothing at all mnemonic.

I used pe'i, BTW, because all of the good words that could be tied to=20
jinvi were already assigned.  Yes, it is probably malglico, but it=20
probably can still be mnemonically useful even for a non-English=20
speaker. (on the possibly faulty assumption that most opinions derive=20
from thinking about the subject being opined about %^).

